Hey,

`getClient()` initializes the AWS Bedrock client on-demand. The function relies on the AWS SDK Credentials Provider Chain to resolve some AWS access/secret keys, and it does it through a temporary environment using `withTempEnv`. If you look at what `withTempEnv` does, it modifies `process.env` very temporarily - for just enough time such that the SDK finishes its job. After that, the keys are passed to the AWS calls, and there is no other calls to the AWS using undefined credentials. As such, I don't think we need to permanently modify `process.env`, which may affect other VSCode extensions as well.

I believe the reason for the problem you are facing lies in the following code path:

The temporary environment is set like this:
```
const credentials = await AwsBedrockHandler.withTempEnv(
	() => {
		AwsBedrockHandler.setEnv("AWS_REGION", this.options.awsRegion)
		AwsBedrockHandler.setEnv("AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID", this.options.awsAccessKey)
		AwsBedrockHandler.setEnv("AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY", this.options.awsSecretKey)
		AwsBedrockHandler.setEnv("AWS_SESSION_TOKEN", this.options.awsSessionToken)
		AwsBedrockHandler.setEnv("AWS_PROFILE", this.options.awsProfile)
	},
	() => providerChain(),
)
```

So not only does it re-use whatever may be in `process.env`, but reading `setEnv` we find that it will not (for example) change `AWS_PROFILE` if `this.options.awsProfile` is undefined:

```
private static async setEnv(key: string, value: string | undefined) {
	if (key !== "" && value !== undefined) {
		process.env[key] = value
	}
}
```

So assuming you do not set the AWS Profile option in Cline - only some keys, but you do have AWS_PROFILE in your env.. you will have something quite unexpected.

---

Assuming Cline does not want to support the case where AWS credentials are provided in the VSCode's env (which I don't personally think it would given the sheer existence of the configuration pane), I would imagine that you could fix the issue without further side-effects by adding a single line to `withTempEnv`:

```
private static async withTempEnv<R>(updateEnv: () => void, fn: () => Promise<R>): Promise<R> {
	const previousEnv = { ...process.env }

	try {
+		process.env = {}
		updateEnv()
		return await fn()
	} finally {
		process.env = previousEnv
	}
}
```

---

To answer your concern above the previous change, there is no particular reason `withTempEnv` and `setEnv` are the way they are specifically, the logic was borrowed from [Anthropic's own SDK](https://github.com/anthropics/anthropic-sdk-typescript/blob/61b55599d50d9c93840e4736cb756cb3a62b0696/packages/bedrock-sdk/src/auth.ts#L21). The previous change is more about ensuring that the credentials and the client are resolved lazily and on-demand - not only on start of the application (such that we can renew SSO sessions, etc).

I think your change would very much simplify the usage of Cline by removing a painful (and not necessarily obvious) edge-case.

